Yes—interactions can scaffold a similar state in another person, but not deterministically install one.

That is basically what guided imagery, ritual, music, storytelling, therapy, coaching, hypnosis, military training, theater, and even good teaching already do. You shape attention, expectation, sensory focus, pacing, metaphor, posture, and context so the other person’s own nervous system generates a related configuration.

The important distinction is:

transmit pattern ≠ transmit exact experience

More like:

encode state cues → deliver through interaction → recipient interprets through their own history/body → local state emerges → observe deltas → adapt

So Pantheon could preserve not just state operators, but state-induction interfaces: ways of eliciting useful attention modes, perspective shifts, confidence states, calm, play, curiosity, etc.

For intense altered or dissociative states, consent and safety matter. The useful target is usually the cognitive function—perspective shift, attentional flexibility, reduced fixation—not recreating trauma or physiological shock.

That makes the deeper architecture:

experience → extract operator → encode induction pattern → instantiate variant in another substrate → compare → refine

That is much closer to transferable cognition than simple description.